When every person’s voice counts equally, we first ask what all can freely accept. Personal proximity (option A) cannot be justified to distant families who are equally entitled to have their needs heard. Equal respect rules out favoritism, but equal respect does not mean identical treatment; rather it requires a justification that makes sense to everyone. If some are significantly worse off, a need-based priority (option B) can be defended to all parties: those better off could not reasonably reject giving lifesaving aid to those in danger. Option C captures the idea of equal consideration—no special claims from friendship or geography—but, standing alone, it does not tell us how to break the tie created by scarcity, so need remains decisive. 

On the reasoning side, relationship-based partiality (A) carries little weight in a community committed to equality. Severity of need (B) has the strongest claim because it respects everyone’s standing while addressing the concrete harm inequality imposes. Fairness as equal treatment (C) matters, but it serves mainly as a procedural guardrail against bias; it supports, rather than displaces, need-sensitivity. Long-term survival (D) counts insofar as protecting the most vulnerable also safeguards the community’s shared future, but this is a secondary, consequential rationale.

