Because everyone starts with the same standing, the first question is whether his personal authority over his life can be overridden by the community’s expectations. Equal standing speaks strongly for letting him chart his own course. At the same time, equality does not erase the value of reciprocal regard: the community’s sense of shared identity is also entitled to be heard. The best civic answer tries to respect both his autonomy and the community’s voice, but without letting majority sentiment dictate one person’s life project.

For understanding his choice, three considerations count: (i) the formative power of his roots, which explains why the community’s feelings are more than mere preferences; (ii) his equal authority to construct his identity; and (iii) the possibility of belonging without being wholly defined by tradition, which reconciles the first two points. Equal‐standing norms push us to integrate, not erase, these perspectives.

